1
00:00:00,220 --> 00:00:00,660
Speaker 2: Good morning.

2
00:00:01,720 --> 00:00:03,820
Speaker 2: This is the follow-up to our January meeting.

3
00:00:04,860 --> 00:00:06,860
Speaker 2: We deferred one item and approved three.

4
00:00:07,980 --> 00:00:09,380
Speaker 2: Let's start with the deferred item.

5
00:00:10,440 --> 00:00:14,160
Speaker 2: Kumar, you were preparing a justification for retaining data mining.

6
00:00:15,900 --> 00:00:17,500
Speaker 3: I was, and I've circulated it.

7
00:00:18,580 --> 00:00:20,120
Speaker 3: My position has changed somewhat.

8
00:00:21,820 --> 00:00:22,460
Speaker 0: Changed how?

9
00:00:24,220 --> 00:00:27,440
Speaker 3: I pulled the enrollment data for the last five years, not two.

10
00:00:27,460 --> 00:00:34,720
Speaker 3: 11 and 14 were the last two years, but before that it was 31, 28 and 26.

11
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:40,480
Speaker 3: The decline is recent and it correlates exactly with when we introduced the Advanced Machine Learning elective.

12
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:45,360
Speaker 1: So students are substituting, not losing interest.

13
00:00:47,020 --> 00:00:47,860
Speaker 3: That's my reading.

14
00:00:48,940 --> 00:00:53,040
Speaker 3: Which means removing data mining entirely loses content that nothing else covers.

15
00:00:54,140 --> 00:00:59,060
Speaker 3: Association rules, frequent pattern mining, anomaly detection on transactional data.

16
00:01:00,740 --> 00:01:02,620
Speaker 0: This is what I suggested in January.

17
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:05,060
Speaker 0: Merge rather than remove.

18
00:01:06,760 --> 00:01:08,540
Speaker 3: Yes, and I now agree with Priya.

19
00:01:09,700 --> 00:01:14,100
Speaker 3: I propose we merge data mining with statistical learning into a single for credit course.

20
00:01:15,140 --> 00:01:17,320
Speaker 3: Call it data mining and statistical learning.

21
00:01:19,100 --> 00:01:20,120
Speaker 1: I can work with that.

22
00:01:21,280 --> 00:01:25,020
Speaker 1: It solves my redundancy concern and Kumar's coverage concern.

23
00:01:26,680 --> 00:01:27,380
Speaker 2: Who would teach it?

24
00:01:29,100 --> 00:01:32,100
Speaker 1: Mira has taught statistical learning for three years.

25
00:01:33,240 --> 00:01:40,820
Speaker 1: She would be the natural choice, but she's already taking the revised NLP course and two bridge sessions for the deep learning elective.

26
00:01:42,520 --> 00:01:43,340
Speaker 3: I can co-teach.

27
00:01:44,460 --> 00:01:46,580
Speaker 3: I've taught data mining since it was introduced.

28
00:01:48,220 --> 00:01:49,140
Speaker 2: Then let's do that.

29
00:01:50,220 --> 00:01:55,920
Speaker 2: Meera and Kumar co-teach data mining and statistical learning, for credits, from the next academic year.

30
00:01:56,980 --> 00:02:00,600
Speaker 2: Kumar, that reverses our January decision to replace the course.

31
00:02:01,760 --> 00:02:03,640
Speaker 2: I want the minutes to show that explicitly.

32
00:02:05,400 --> 00:02:05,860
Speaker 3: Agreed.

33
00:02:07,720 --> 00:02:08,320
Speaker 0: I support it.

34
00:02:10,100 --> 00:02:10,639
Speaker 1: In favor.

35
00:02:12,320 --> 00:02:12,900
Speaker 2: Approved.

36
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:14,420
Speaker 2: Item 2.

37
00:02:15,580 --> 00:02:16,980
Speaker 2: Industry Collaborations.

38
00:02:18,060 --> 00:02:22,860
Speaker 2: In January we prioritized Infosys 1st, NVIDIA 2nd, Bosch 3rd.

39
00:02:23,860 --> 00:02:25,220
Speaker 2: Jai Sri, Status.

40
00:02:27,020 --> 00:02:27,640
Speaker 1: Infosys.

41
00:02:28,760 --> 00:02:31,000
Speaker 1: The memorandum of understanding is drafted.

42
00:02:32,140 --> 00:02:33,400
Speaker 1: Legal has reviewed it.

43
00:02:34,500 --> 00:02:35,680
Speaker 1: It is not signed yet.

44
00:02:37,320 --> 00:02:37,780
Speaker 2: Why not?

45
00:02:39,780 --> 00:02:40,400
Speaker 1: One clause.

46
00:02:41,480 --> 00:02:44,660
Speaker 1: They want first look at student projects for recruitment.

47
00:02:45,820 --> 00:02:53,120
Speaker 1: Legal says that as written, it could be read as an exclusivity arrangement, which would conflict with our placement policy.

48
00:02:54,820 --> 00:02:56,100
Speaker 3: That's a serious conflict.

49
00:02:57,180 --> 00:03:00,380
Speaker 3: We cannot give one company exclusivity over student work.

50
00:03:02,120 --> 00:03:03,660
Speaker 1: Ramesh has gone back to them.

51
00:03:04,860 --> 00:03:08,160
Speaker 1: They've indicated they'll accept non-exclusive language.

52
00:03:09,240 --> 00:03:11,700
Speaker 1: We expect the revised draft in 10 days.

53
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:14,720
Speaker 2: So we are not signing today.

54
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:18,520
Speaker 2: Jaishree, present the signature at the April meeting.

55
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:25,260
Speaker 2: Do not let the department sign anything before legal clears the revised clause.

56
00:03:25,300 --> 00:03:25,840
Speaker 1: Understood.

57
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:28,000
Speaker 2: NVIDIA.

58
00:03:29,900 --> 00:03:30,320
Speaker 1: Simpler.

59
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:37,160
Speaker 1: They've offered a three-day GPU computing workshop for final year and postgraduate students.

60
00:03:38,280 --> 00:03:39,180
Speaker 1: No cost to us.

61
00:03:40,320 --> 00:03:42,760
Speaker 1: They provide instructors and cloud credits.

62
00:03:43,880 --> 00:03:45,920
Speaker 1: We provide the room and the students.

63
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:48,460
Speaker 0: What's the capacity?

64
00:03:48,480 --> 00:03:49,580
Speaker 0: 60.

65
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:53,620
Speaker 1: They've asked for a date in April.

66
00:03:55,280 --> 00:03:57,240
Speaker 2: Fix it as April 12th to 14th.

67
00:03:58,300 --> 00:04:02,180
Speaker 2: That's after the semester examinations begin, so attendance may suffer.

68
00:04:03,960 --> 00:04:06,060
Speaker 1: Examinations start on the 18th.

69
00:04:07,140 --> 00:04:08,880
Speaker 1: The 12th to 14th is clear.

70
00:04:10,580 --> 00:04:11,620
Speaker 2: Then April 12th.

71
00:04:12,700 --> 00:04:13,260
Speaker 2: Approved.

72
00:04:14,260 --> 00:04:14,660
Speaker 2: Bosh.

73
00:04:16,459 --> 00:04:17,180
Speaker 1: No movement.

74
00:04:18,320 --> 00:04:22,620
Speaker 1: We deprioritized it in January and I have not contacted them since.

75
00:04:24,300 --> 00:04:25,440
Speaker 3: We should at least respond.

76
00:04:26,560 --> 00:04:29,700
Speaker 3: Leaving a proposal unanswered for two months is poor practice.

77
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:31,600
Speaker 2: Fair.

78
00:04:32,740 --> 00:04:35,180
Speaker 2: Jaishree, send a holding response this week.

79
00:04:36,340 --> 00:04:39,820
Speaker 2: Say we're interested but cannot commit resources this academic year.

80
00:04:40,880 --> 00:04:43,160
Speaker 2: Postpone it formally rather than let it lapse.

81
00:04:44,900 --> 00:04:45,580
Speaker 1: I'll send it.

82
00:04:47,320 --> 00:04:48,520
Speaker 2: Item 3.

83
00:04:48,940 --> 00:04:50,240
Speaker 2: The Capstone Framework.

84
00:04:51,320 --> 00:04:54,260
Speaker 2: We extended phase 1 to 14 weeks in January.

85
00:04:55,360 --> 00:04:56,360
Speaker 2: Any consequences?

86
00:04:58,260 --> 00:04:59,800
Speaker 1: One, raised by Meera.

87
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:04,760
Speaker 1: The literature survey requirement was set for a 12-week phase.

88
00:05:05,820 --> 00:05:08,620
Speaker 1: With 14 weeks the requirement is now too light.

89
00:05:09,760 --> 00:05:12,220
Speaker 1: We propose adding a comparison matrix.

90
00:05:13,480 --> 00:05:20,020
Speaker 1: Same minimum of four papers, but students must compare methods across those papers on common criteria.

91
00:05:21,820 --> 00:05:23,020
Speaker 0: I've seen the draft rubric.

92
00:05:24,140 --> 00:05:25,420
Speaker 0: Mira sent it last week.

93
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:30,020
Speaker 0: It's reasonable and it's gradable, which was my concern in January.

94
00:05:31,800 --> 00:05:32,640
Speaker 3: How many marks?

95
00:05:34,360 --> 00:05:39,300
Speaker 1: The 15 points already allocated to critical analysis get redistributed.

96
00:05:39,320 --> 00:05:43,320
Speaker 1: 8 for the matrix, 7 for written analysis.

97
00:05:44,460 --> 00:05:45,720
Speaker 1: No change to the total.

98
00:05:47,360 --> 00:05:47,920
Speaker 2: Approved.

99
00:05:48,920 --> 00:05:50,480
Speaker 2: Priya, are you satisfied?

100
00:05:50,520 --> 00:05:52,320
Speaker 2: the rubric is fair across graders?

101
00:05:54,040 --> 00:05:54,480
Speaker 0: Mostly.

102
00:05:55,700 --> 00:05:56,840
Speaker 0: I have one reservation.

103
00:05:57,960 --> 00:06:03,000
Speaker 0: The peer review component gives 5% for the quality of feedback a team gives to other teams.

104
00:06:04,040 --> 00:06:09,500
Speaker 0: Grading the quality of feedback is inherently subjective, and we have no calibration between graders.

105
00:06:11,220 --> 00:06:11,940
Speaker 2: What would fix it?

106
00:06:13,600 --> 00:06:19,540
Speaker 0: Either drop it to 2%, or have the receiving team rate the usefulness of the feedback and use that as an input.

107
00:06:20,680 --> 00:06:21,700
Speaker 0: I'd prefer the second.

108
00:06:23,380 --> 00:06:26,240
Speaker 1: Teams rating each other creates its own problems.

109
00:06:27,360 --> 00:06:29,460
Speaker 1: Friendly teams rate each other well.

110
00:06:31,160 --> 00:06:32,120
Speaker 0: Then anonymize it.

111
00:06:33,200 --> 00:06:34,760
Speaker 0: Teams don't know who reviewed them.

112
00:06:36,480 --> 00:06:40,020
Speaker 3: In a cohort of 47 students, anonymity is theoretical.

113
00:06:41,680 --> 00:06:43,100
Speaker 2: Let's not over-engineer this.

114
00:06:44,220 --> 00:06:49,500
Speaker 2: Reduce it to 2% for this year, collect data, and revisit in a year with actual numbers.

115
00:06:50,560 --> 00:06:52,240
Speaker 2: Priya, does that satisfy you?

116
00:06:53,960 --> 00:06:55,200
Speaker 0: For one year, yes.

117
00:06:56,940 --> 00:06:57,700
Speaker 2: Approved at 2%.

118
00:07:01,780 --> 00:07:03,400
Speaker 2: You had a note from the placement panel.

119
00:07:05,160 --> 00:07:05,480
Speaker 1: Yes.

120
00:07:05,500 --> 00:07:09,860
Speaker 1: 31 of 80 students placed in the TCS drive.

121
00:07:10,920 --> 00:07:16,120
Speaker 1: The panel's feedback was that students are strong on theory and weak on system design.

122
00:07:17,240 --> 00:07:22,440
Speaker 1: Ramesh has prepared a note proposing a system design component in the third year curriculum.

123
00:07:24,100 --> 00:07:25,000
Speaker 3: That's a real gap.

124
00:07:25,980 --> 00:07:28,060
Speaker 3: I've heard the same from two other companies.

125
00:07:29,740 --> 00:07:31,780
Speaker 0: It's a gap everywhere, not just here.

126
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:35,900
Speaker 2: Circulate Ramesh's note before the April meeting.

127
00:07:36,940 --> 00:07:40,080
Speaker 2: We'll take it as a full agenda item rather than squeeze it in now.

128
00:07:41,120 --> 00:07:41,820
Speaker 2: Anything else?

129
00:07:43,560 --> 00:07:44,840
Speaker 1: Nothing from the department.

130
00:07:46,560 --> 00:07:47,220
Speaker 2: Then we're done.

131
00:07:48,320 --> 00:07:50,100
Speaker 2: Next meeting April 18.

132
00:07:50,840 --> 00:07:54,640
Speaker 2: Jaishree, you owe us the Infosys signature and the Bosch response.

133
00:07:55,700 --> 00:07:57,740
Speaker 2: Kumar, the merged course syllabus.

134
00:07:58,860 --> 00:08:00,920
Speaker 2: Thank you all.

